[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Native SQL Queries and ResultSet Mapping in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"While JPA and JPQL abstract database-specific SQL to provide portability, there are scenarios where you need the full power of database-specific SQL features. JPA provides mechanisms to execute native SQL queries and map the results back to entities, scalar values, or custom DTOs using ResultSet Mapping."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Executing Basic Native Queries"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can execute raw SQL by using the EntityManager.createNativeQuery() method. If the query maps directly to an existing managed entity, you pass the entity class as the second parameter."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE status = 'ACTIVE'";
List<User> activeUsers = entityManager.createNativeQuery(sql, User.class).getResultList();"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Using SqlResultSetMapping for Complex Mappings"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When your native query returns columns that do not map directly to a single entity structure, or when you need to combine multiple entities and scalar values, you must use the @SqlResultSetMapping annotation."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@SqlResultSetMapping(
    name = "UserSummaryMapping",
    classes = @ConstructorResult(
        targetClass = com.example.dto.UserSummaryDto.class,
        columns = {
            @ColumnResult(name = "id", type = Long.class),
            @ColumnResult(name = "username", type = String.class),
            @ColumnResult(name = "order_count", type = Integer.class)
        }
    )
)
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    // Entity definitions
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Executing the Named ResultSet Mapping"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the result set mapping is defined on an entity, you can reference it by name in your createNativeQuery call."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"String sql = "SELECT u.id, u.username, COUNT(o.id) as order_count FROM users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id GROUP BY u.id, u.username";

List<UserSummaryDto> summaries = entityManager
    .createNativeQuery(sql, "UserSummaryMapping")
    .getResultList();"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Benefits and Best Practices"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bypass JPQL limitations when utilizing database-specific functions or performance optimizations."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use @ConstructorResult when projecting native queries directly into DTOs to ensure type safety."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be cautious of database portability issues when writing native SQL, as changing the underlying database may require rewriting queries."}]
